course: money
lesson: 18
title: Mum's card, in-app buys and subscriptions that renew
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The "buy" button in a game or app on a family tablet is not pretend: it usually uses a real bank card saved on the account, so every tap spends real money, and Sophie's 6 taps at £4.99 cost £29.94.
explain: Using someone's card without asking is not okay, even in a family: Tyler's £50 of game coins is what his mum spends on food for three or four days, and the shop does not always give money back.
explain: A free trial often turns into a paid subscription if nobody cancels it: Leo forgot a cartoon app, so £5.99 left Dad's account every month, £11.98 in two months for an app nobody opened.
explain: Simple habits keep money safe: ask before you buy, read every pop-up before pressing, turn off "remember card", use a gift card with a set limit, and if you do get it wrong, own up quickly and pay it back.
fact: An in-app purchase is buying something inside a game or app with real money.
fact: Tablets and consoles often have a parent's card saved, so a tap on "buy" spends real money.
fact: Spending money from someone's card without permission is called an unauthorised payment; it is not okay, even in a family.
fact: App shops sometimes refund mistakes made by children, but not always.
fact: Parental controls are settings that stop children buying, for example by asking for a password.
fact: A subscription is paying regularly, often every month, for something like music, videos or a game.
fact: A free trial often renews into a paid subscription automatically unless you cancel it in time; companies count on people forgetting.
fact: Small monthly subscriptions add up to big amounts over a year, so it is worth checking and cancelling unused ones.
fact: A gift card or top-up has a set amount, so you cannot spend more than is on it.
fact: Turning off a saved card means the full card number must be typed in, which stops accidental buys.
fact: Owning up quickly and paying back money you spent without asking helps rebuild trust.
exercise: What is an in-app purchase? | Buying something inside a game or app with real money | A free gift inside a game | A pretend button that does nothing | Downloading an app for free
exercise: Sophie tapped "buy" 6 times at £4.99 each. How much did she spend? | £29.94 | £24.95 | £30.00 | £29.99
exercise: Why wasn't the buy button pretend? | It used a real card saved on the account | All tablets print money | The game was broken | It was a free trial
exercise: Tyler spent £50 on Mum's card without asking. What is this called? | An unauthorised payment | A normal monthly subscription | A refund from the app store | A free trial that ran out early
exercise: Mum's son spent £40 by mistake and the app shop refunded £30. How much was lost? | £10 | £30 | £70 | £40
exercise: What are parental controls? | Settings that stop children buying without permission | Rules about bedtime | Buttons for parents to buy faster | A list of games parents like
exercise: Leo's forgotten app takes £5.99 a month. How much in 2 months? | £11.98 | £5.99 | £12.00 | £11.99
exercise: Why do free trials often turn into paid subscriptions? | They renew automatically unless you cancel | The law says every free trial must cost money | The app stops working if you don't pay for it | Free trials are never really free from the start
exercise: Dad's subscriptions are £10, £8, £6 and £5 a month. What is the total? | £29 | £19 | £30 | £24
exercise: Dad only uses the £10 one. How much a month is wasted? | £19 | £29 | £10 | £9
exercise: £19 wasted a month for 12 months. How much is that in a year? | £228 | £190 | £219 | £120
exercise: Ruby has £8 and buys a £3 add-on with her own money, after asking. How much is left? | £5 | £11 | £3 | £8
exercise: Josh's gift card has £10 a month. He spends £8 in week one. How much is left for the month? | £2 | £8 | £18 | £10
exercise: Why does turning off "remember card" help? | Buying needs the full card number typed in | It makes the card worth more | It cancels all subscriptions | It gives free coins
exercise: Finn sees "Confirm purchase £19.99?" and closes it. How much did he save? | £19.99 | £20.00 | £1.99 | £0
exercise: Nora spent £15 without asking and pays back £3 a week. How many weeks? | 5 | 3 | 15 | 12
test: Sophie thought the buy button was "pretend, like a toy shop". What should she know now? | Buy buttons often spend real money from a saved card | Buttons are only real on phones | Tablets can't spend money | Only grown-ups' taps cost money
test: Mum sees her bank app and goes white: "TYLER! Get down here NOW!" He'd spent £50 on coins. What should Tyler do? | Own up, say sorry and help pay it back | Say the dog did it and hope nobody checks | Spend a bit more so it all looks normal | Hide the tablet until Mum forgets about it
test: Dad: "bloody hell, four subscriptions, I only use one!" What's the smart move? | Cancel the ones he doesn't use | Keep them all in case | Add one more for luck | Ignore it, it's only small amounts
test: Leo's free trial: he forgot, and £5.99 left Dad's account every month for 5 months. How much? | £29.95 | £29.99 | £30.00 | £5.99
test: Josh's mum says "when it's gone, it's gone, kiddo" about his £10 gift card. What's good about it? | He can never spend more than £10 a month | He gets more money when it runs out | It links to Mum's card for top-ups | It turns into a subscription
test: Ruby asks first and Mum says "thanks for asking, yes." Why does asking matter? | It keeps trust and nobody gets a shock | Asking makes the add-on cheaper | Mum would never notice anyway | The game requires a parent's signature
test: Finn's game flashes a big bright "YES" button. Dad says "good lad, always read before you press." Why are buttons big and bright? | To get you to tap without thinking | To help people who can't see very well | Because games just love bright colours | So you can find the exit button quickly
test: Mum rings the app shop: "Lucky this time." What would happen if she hadn't been lucky? | The money might not come back at all | The shop would pay double the money back | The game would refund itself after a week | The bank would cancel the whole game for you
test: Nora feels awful and tells Mum herself about the £15. A Gen Alpha cousin says "she ate, no crumbs" ("ate" means did it brilliantly). Why is owning up good? | Mum is cross but proud, and trust starts to come back | It means Nora doesn't have to pay it back | It makes the gems free | It's the same as never having done it
test: Amelia's bro turns off "remember card" on the family laptop. Before, one click = £5.99. Now what's needed? | Typing the whole 16-digit card number | Nothing, because it still takes one click | A selfie of a parent holding the bank card | A phone call to the bank to check it's you
test: What would happen if a family had 3 forgotten £5 subscriptions for a whole year? | They'd lose £180 | They'd lose £15 | They'd lose £60 | They'd lose nothing
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a "subscription"? | An arrangement for regular, usually monthly, payment in respect of a service | A single payment for a game which is never repeated | A refund furnished by an application store | A gift card of fixed value
exam: It is given that a purchase of four pounds ninety-nine pence is effected six times. Compute the aggregate expenditure. | Twenty-nine pounds ninety-four pence | Twenty-four pounds ninety-five pence | Thirty pounds | Twenty-nine pounds ninety-nine pence
exam: It is given that subscriptions of ten, eight, six and five pounds are payable monthly and that only the first is used. Ascertain the sum wasted per annum. | Two hundred and twenty-eight pounds | One hundred and ninety pounds | Three hundred and forty-eight pounds | One hundred and twenty pounds
exam: Identify the course of action most consistent with the established guidance where a child has expended a parent's money without permission. | Prompt admission and repayment of the sum | Concealment of the matter from the parent | Further expenditure to obscure the first | Attribution of responsibility to a sibling
